\subtree{
  \title{Decision}
  \p{The category of double graphs is \code{PSh(G_fc)} where \code{G_fc} has objects \code{pt}, \code{v} (vertical edge), \code{h} (horizontal edge), \code{c_n} (cell with \code{n ≥ 0} inputs and one output), \code{c_n^∅} (cell with \code{n ≥ 0} inputs and empty output, the augmented cells), and generating maps \code{s,t : pt → v}, \code{s,t : pt → h}, \code{in_i : h → c_n} (\code{1 ≤ i ≤ n}), \code{out : h → c_n}, \code{l, r : v → c_n} (left and right sides), the same for \code{c_n^∅} without \code{out}, subject to the incidence relations (input \code{i} ends where input \code{i+1} starts; the left side runs from the start of input 1 to the start of the output; the right side from the end of input \code{n} to the end of the output; for \code{n = 0} the single input object is shared by both sides; for \code{c_n^∅} both sides end at the same object). A set-level augmented VDC is a double graph with vertical composition of vertical edges, multicategorical composition of cells (including Koudenburg's rule for cells with empty target), unit vertical edges, unit cells, and the associativity/unit laws. The free augmented VDC on a double graph is constructed explicitly. \code{Θ^aug_fc := Θ_T} for this \code{T}.}
  \p{\strong{Expected description (\ref{at-kr-0003}): leveled grids, not trees.} Composition of cells requires adjacent children to \em{share} vertical sides (\code{α_i}'s right side is \code{α_{i+1}}'s left side). In a free VDC a generating cell has generating sides, so it can never sit next to an identity cell (identity sides) or next to a composite (sides of length ≥ 2). Hence in the arities every node has all inputs filled or none, all leaves are at the same depth, and a pasting scheme is a stack of \strong{rows}: a path of \code{n} horizontal edges, partitioned into consecutive blocks with one cell per block, producing the next path, down to a single output (or, augmented, no output). A block of size 0 is a nullary-input cell in a gap; a block without output is a Koudenburg cell. The vertical data is determined: one vertical edge per block boundary per row. Equivalently a cell shape is a composable chain of \em{active} maps in Δ (a row with \code{k} blocks on a path of length \code{n} is an endpoint-preserving monotone \code{[k] → [n]}), which is why style A lives over \code{Δᵒᵖ}. Objects of \code{Θ^aug_fc}: \code{pt}, \code{v^n} (\code{n ≥ 1}), \code{h}, and grids. Faces: sub-grids (a row interval times a column interval closed under blocks; inert) and merging of consecutive rows (active); degeneracies: inserting identity rows and collapsing vertical edges. Everything is Δ-data with no automorphisms, which makes elegance (\ref{at-kr-0006}) expected rather than hoped. \strong{(verify: the shared-sides argument is Cruttwell–Shulman's definition read literally; the exact face/degeneracy description is a task.)} Note that \code{Ω_p} trees are \em{not} leveled: multicategory cells have no sides. The earlier draft's "planar trees of cells with vertical sides" was wrong for exactly this reason.}
}
\subtree{
  \title{Rationale}
  \p{The shapes are what the algebra says they are. Their simplicity is a feature: the canonical form is a list of rows of block sizes (→ \ref{dec-tl-0010}), and the pasting calculus is decidable equality of such data (→ \ref{dec-tl-0009}).}
}
\subtree{
  \title{Acceptance}
  \p{\ref{at-kr-0008}: the nerve theorem for augmented VDCs is an instance of \ref{at-kr-0002} and its essential image is characterized by the Segal condition of \ref{dec-kr-0004}. \ref{at-kr-0012}: typed leveled 2-molecules of the geometric substrate (→ \ref{dec-kr-0012}) are exactly the grids (both directions).}
}
\subtree{
  \title{Status}
  \p{frozen (the exact list of incidence relations and the face/degeneracy description are provisional until formalized).}
}
